data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining the Search Scope**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the search scope, aiming for rare, high-value objects. This involves scientific hypotheses, computer programs, and mathematical statements. I'm trying to refine my understanding of the problem space's vastness and the constraints within it, making it more manageable for the search process. I'm focusing on clarifying the \"high-value\" criteria.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 658,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 70,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining the Framework**\n\nI am now structuring the analysis around six specific sub-questions to ensure a more thorough investigation. I'm implementing strict meta-constraints, including explicit citations and separating known facts from hypotheses and cited memory. The goal is to provide transparency and reliability. I must also include contrary results if they exist. Furthermore, I will use French to match the high scholarly register.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 899,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 311,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying Citations**\n\nI'm now refining the citation methodology for this project. I've broken it down into three distinct categories: **[Savoir établi]** for verified mathematical truths and core literature facts; **[Cité de mémoire]** for precise recalls from training data like titles, dates, or metrics; and **[Supposition]** for my analytical extrapolations and structural syntheses. Every single numerical claim must be sourced, and I'll keep the numbers minimal and rigorous, prioritizing well-known benchmarks.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1119,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 531,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Search Strategies**\n\nI'm now comparing guided versus unguided exploration methods. I'm looking at Evolutionary Search, MCTS, Bandits, and Bayesian Optimization against i.i.d. sampling, random walks, and Luby restarts. The \"No Free Lunch Theorems\" suggest uniform functions negate guided algorithms, but I'm focusing on structural regularity and properties like smoothness, Lipschitz continuity, and submodularity. Bayesian Optimization's connection to Gaussian Processes interests me.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1355,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 767,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Search Efficiency**\n\nI'm now deep-diving into the efficiency of my search methods. I'm focusing on regret bounds and information gain, particularly how they scale with dimensionality. Standard kernels seem problematic, so I'm investigating alternative approaches. I'm reviewing literature on tree search, including UCT, and its performance on deceptive trees. I'm also examining Evolutionary Algorithms, particularly the work of Droste, Jansen, and Wegener.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1572,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 984,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Unguided Search**\n\nI'm currently thinking about when unguided search strategies like random restarts or i.i.d. sampling might outperform more complex, guided methods. My current focus is on scenarios like needle-in-a-haystack problems, those with extreme sparsity or deceptiveness, and those with high effective dimensionality and irrelevant variables. I am analyzing the impact of heavy-tailed runtime distributions, and recalling Bergstra and Bengio's work on random search for hyperparameter optimization, and Luby's work on restarts. The $(1+1)$-EA's limitations in these situations are also prominent in my thinking.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1862,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1274,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Restart Advantages**\n\nI'm now integrating the understanding of Las Vegas algorithm speedups, specifically the O(t* log t*) expected time achieved by universal restart strategies, which seems better than un-restarted search based on the Gomes et al. paper from 1998. Brown et al. and Snell et al.'s findings on repeated sampling's effectiveness over guided strategies at equal compute budgets for formal tasks with strong verifiers is also relevant.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2118,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1530,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Comparing Search Strategies**\n\nI am now focusing on the comparison between \"best-of-N\" (unguided parallel) and beam search/lookahead (guided) strategies, particularly concerning very challenging problems. I've noted that independent sampling and sequential refinement can diverge in performance based on difficulty. AlphaCode's success, which relies on extensive independent sample generation and filtering, supports this. I'm also exploring work on hyperparameter optimization (HPO), specifically Hyperband and BOHB, where random search with early stopping demonstrated advantages over Bayesian optimization in certain high-dimensional scenarios.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2405,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1817,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating FunSearch Results**\n\nI'm now integrating recent results from Aredes et al. on FunSearch, published in Nature. Their explicit ablation study provides critical insight. Evolutionary search outperforms pure LLM sampling, but I need to be careful with exact numbers, particularly around the cap set problem in $\\mathbb{Z}_3^8$, which FunSearch solved at 512! I am also rechecking the previous state of the art.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2724,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2136,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Integrating Recent Results**\n\nI'm now integrating recent findings, particularly from Aredes et al.'s FunSearch in Nature. I need to explicitly verify the cap set problem solution; the 512 size in 8 dimensions is crucial `[Cité de mémoire]`. I'm comparing this to Trinh et al.'s neuro-symbolic search, Fawzi et al.'s RL-guided MCTS, and Shields et al.'s Bayesian optimization success. Lehman & Stanley's novelty search is also relevant.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3025,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2437,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Novelty Dynamics**\n\nI'm now diving into the dynamics of novelty, specifically exploring models beyond simple search. Kauffman's \"Adjacent Possible\" and Tria et al.'s work on correlated novelties are relevant, particularly Polya's urn models. I'm focusing on the mathematical derivation of Heaps' law and Zipf's law and how they relate to the growth of distinct novelties in real-world data like Wikipedia edits. I need to understand how well these models capture the clustering of novelties.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3297,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2709,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Novelty Growth**\n\nI'm now incorporating recent economic models of endogenous R&D and combinatorial growth. Romer (1990) and Weitzman (1998) suggest that the key bottleneck is processing capacity due to the super-exponential growth of combinations of ideas. Jones (1995) adds that \"fishing-out\" is key to diminishing returns. Bloom et al. (2020) empirically supports this and notes that ideas are getting harder to find. I'm focusing on Heaps' and Zipf's laws, as well.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3621,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3033,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Diminishing Returns**\n\nI'm now focusing on the implications of diminishing returns in various fields, like semiconductors and medicine. Bloom et al.'s 2020 paper, `[Cité de mémoire]`, underscores the significant increase in researchers needed to sustain Moore's Law. I am also recalling how Kortum's 1997 work modeled idea search as a Fréchet distribution. I'm investigating phase transitions in random graphs and their relevance to knowledge network percolation.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3894,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3306,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Complexity Dynamics**\n\nI'm now integrating complexity dynamics by revisiting concepts of phase transitions in computational problems. Monasson et al. (1999) and Mézard et al. (2002) offer models for random satisfiability problems. Achlioptas & Coja-Oghlan (2008) offer clustering/shattering transition concepts. Additionally, I am also reviewing rugged landscapes, Kauffman & Levin's (1987) $NK$ model and Fleming & Sorenson's (2001) work on patent data.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4158,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3570,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Networked Innovation**\n\nI am now focusing on recent research on networked innovation, specifically regarding atypical combinations and their impact. Uzzi et al.'s 2013 *Science* paper, which examined 17.9 million Web of Science papers, revealed that highly impactful papers tend to combine conventional knowledge with novel, atypical combinations. This finding aligns with Foster et al.'s 2015 work on biomedical abstracts and chemical reaction networks, which models scientists' strategic risk-taking.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4415,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3827,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Idea Evolution**\n\nI'm now integrating branching process models, such as Galton-Watson and Hawkes self-exciting processes, to understand idea and citation cascades. Golosovsky & Solomon's 2012 PRL paper and Wang, Song, & Barabási's 2013 *Science* paper on citation networks are crucial. Sinatra, Wang, Deville, Song, & Barabási's 2016 *Science* paper provides a contrary view with the \"random impact rule\". I need to understand the interplay between these models.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4687,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4099,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Integrating Interdisciplinary Perspectives**\n\nI'm now integrating insights from other fields, differentiating between empirical data and narrative accounts, separating \"Ce qui est mesuré\" from \"Ce qui est raconté\". I am focusing on testimonies from discoverers, such as Poincaré's account of the Fuchsian functions and Hadamard's survey of mathematicians, including Einstein's visual and kinesthetic thinking.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4943,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4355,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Discovery Stages**\n\nI'm now analyzing the classical stages of discovery: preparation, incubation, illumination, and verification, drawing on both scientific and historical data. I'm carefully evaluating the accounts of August Kekulé's dream of the benzene structure, including the historical debate about its veracity; I am currently referencing Wotiz & Rudofsky's (1984) critique of the Kekulé narrative, along with Rocke's (2010) response. I am also recalling Alexander Fleming's penicillin discovery and the scientific debate over his accounts.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5265,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4677,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Discovery Philosophies**\n\nI'm now diving into the philosophical underpinnings of scientific discovery, particularly focusing on the debate around inductive logic versus other modes of reasoning. Reichenbach and Popper's rejection of inductive logic contrasts sharply with Peirce's concept of abduction. Hanson and Simon suggest a rational heuristic, while Kuhn emphasizes the role of accumulated anomalies.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5489,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4901,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Incubation Effects**\n\nI'm now diving into the cognitive psychology of discovery. Sio & Ormerod's meta-analysis confirms incubation's positive effect, especially for divergent problems and lighter tasks during breaks. I am also noting Weisberg's critique, questioning unconscious processes in favor of forgetting fixation, along with Smith & Blankenship's work. Complex analytical problems and short preparation may have limited or negative effects.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5725,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5137,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Laboratory Dynamics**\n\nI'm now integrating Kevin Dunbar's 1995/1997 work on in vivo scientific thinking, contrasting this with in vitro lab tests. The focus is on scientists using local analogies for troubleshooting, contrasting with narratives where distant ones are prevalent. Collective reasoning and failed controls are central, which differs from confirmation bias in isolated individuals. EEG/fMRI insights and creativity are now also under consideration.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6018,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5430,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Neurocognitive Insights**\n\nI'm now integrating neurocognitive data on insight. I've noted gamma activity (~40 Hz, Jung-Beeman et al. 2004) in the right anterior superior temporal gyrus preceding the \"Aha!\" moment. Increased parieto-occipital alpha waves (sensory gating, Kounios & Beeman, 2014) precede this. I'm exploring Beaty et al.'s findings (2016, 2018) on the co-activation of default mode (spontaneous generation) and central executive (evaluation/control) networks, mediated by the salience network, though the ecological validity for scientific discovery is uncertain.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6288,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5700,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Integrating Evolutionary Dynamics**\n\nI'm now integrating evolutionary biology and statistical physics. Andreas Wagner's work (2011) on neutral networks and Lenski et al. (2008) on *E. coli* are in focus. The presence of percolating neutral networks, where a population can drift adaptively at a constant selective value while transforming the accessible phenotypic neighborhood through a single mutation, is of interest.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6547,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 5959,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Contingency Dynamics**\n\nI'm now focusing on the role of historical contingency, drawing on Lenski's LTEE experiment (`[Cité de mémoire]`). Aerobic citrate utilization ($\\text{Cit}^+$) emerged in only one out of twelve populations after thousands of generations (`[Cité de mémoire]`).  I'm now investigating neutral mutations and \"potentiating\" mutations to see how they impact innovation, alongside Lakoff & Johnson and Gentner & Jeziorski's linguistic models.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 6839,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6251,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Discovery Overlaps**\n\nI'm now diving into the interplay between multiple and single scientific discoveries, and I'm interested in the relationship between semantic innovation and subsequent peer uptake. I'm focusing on Merton's 1961 work and Ogburn & Thomas' 1922 paper, specifically their findings on independent, simultaneous inventions. I'm also now analyzing Simonton's work.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7108,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6520,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Cross-Disciplinary Integration**\n\nI'm currently thinking about literature-based discovery, specifically the $A \\to B \\to C$ model presented in a 1986 *Perspectives in Biology and Medicine* article, where the disjoint knowledge of different readerships prevents discovery until connections are made. Ronald Burt's work on \"structural holes\" (2004) is relevant here, along with interdisciplinary integration. I will evaluate factors that increase and decrease the chance of successful innovation.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7336,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6748,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Disciplinary Friction**\n\nI am now focusing on the problems stemming from differing jargon across disciplines, including false synonyms and polysemy. There's an evaluation and funding penalty for interdisciplinary research, specifically lower grant success rates, according to Bromham et al. (2016) and Leahey et al. (2017). A combinatorial explosion of spurious bridging terms, as highlighted by Kostoff et al. (2017), is also being considered.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7571,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 6983,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Epistemic Closure**\n\nI'm now focusing on modeling epistemic closure, contrasting logical deduction with resource-bounded limits. I'm thinking about forgotten lemmas and latent ideas within published material. I'm recalling the potential of word embeddings and formalization for unearthing unstated consequences, with examples such as Tshitoyan et al.'s work and the use of OEIS or Lean to aid in syntactic and semantic searches.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 7784,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7196,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Epistemic Valleys**\n\nI'm currently focused on epistemic valleys, caused by intermediate results being seen as \"trivial\" and thus unpublished. I'm noting the trade-off between dense citation communities supporting short derivation chains, and the need for modular intermediate steps with independent publishable value for longer chains. The role of automated theorem proving, like McCune's 1997 work, is also key. Information overload and fragmentation are becoming clearer.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8025,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7437,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Epistemic Bottlenecks**\n\nI'm now integrating insights on epistemic bottlenecks, especially in high-output fields. I'm focusing on how rapid publication leads to canonization and the neglect of peripheral findings. I'm also examining the effects of tacit knowledge and irreproducible protocols on deductions. My analysis now includes two models, one focusing on distinct classes and another on continuous maxima, given a budget constraint.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8247,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7659,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Future Discoveries**\n\nI'm now calculating future discoveries using the Good-Turing estimator, focusing on McAllester & Schapire's confidence bounds. I'm also exploring the Good & Toulmin work on extrapolation. I'm focusing on adapting these models to the context of novel scientific and mathematical insights.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8504,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 7916,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Calculating Discovery Probabilities**\n\nI'm now focusing on estimating future discoveries and adapting these models for novel scientific insights. I'm exploring the Good-Turing estimator, and am focused on its confidence bounds, as developed by McAllester & Schapire, and also the extrapolation by Good and Toulmin. I'm also investigating the potential for adaptation in this unique field.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 8805,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8217,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Object Probabilities**\n\nI'm now investigating how to handle the uncertainty in object probabilities. I'm focusing on why a lower bound on the probability is needed for effective estimation. I'm also exploring Good-Turing and Chao estimators, especially in the context of fuzzing and program synthesis, and how these models apply to decisions on when to stop. Extreme Value Theory is now also considered.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 9092,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8504,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Modeling Search Dynamics**\n\nI'm now integrating insights on search dynamics, focusing on how to model and manage uncertainty in the search process. I'm deep diving into Extreme Value Theory, especially the Gumbel/Fréchet distributions, and their implications for tail behavior. I'm carefully considering the limitations of assuming i.i.d. draws. Furthermore, I'm integrating Weitzman's optimal search rule and concepts like reservation prices for efficient resource allocation across different generators.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 9365,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 8777,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Extreme Optimization**\n\nI am now focusing on extreme value bandits, optimizing for maximum reward instead of average. I'm considering scenarios where the generator law is known, enabling sampling without replacement or importance sampling. I'm also examining multi-fidelity approaches for budget-efficient verification. The balance between cheap proxies and potential Goodharting effects is crucial.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 9684,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9096,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Falsification Strategies**\n\nI'm now determining falsification methods, particularly focusing on the cost-effectiveness of verification. I'm focusing on benchmark analysis with deterministic formal verifiers and identifying specific scenarios where guided or unguided methods may fail. The cryptographic hash preimage example is becoming a central idea. I am now incorporating the concept of a strictly zero-autocorrelation space, evaluating the impact of such a space on method comparison, and am examining scenarios where the evaluation count B is fixed.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 9930,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9342,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Falsifying Discovery Claims**\n\nI'm now formulating falsification strategies, specifically designed for contrasting guided and unguided search methods. I am focusing on two scenarios: a rigorous comparison of a general-purpose guided algorithm against uniform sampling, and a replication of FunSearch's performance on the cap set problem versus i.i.d. sampling. My focus now also extends to analyzing longitudinal patent/R&D data and demonstrating increased idea per researcher.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10217,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9629,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Formulating Falsification Scenarios**\n\nI am currently formulating falsification strategies to test and contrast different search methodologies. My focus is on scenarios where existing models might fail. I'm investigating specific challenges in ethnographic studies on laboratory meetings and incubation effects. Also, I am planning audits of public grant agency funding records to test interdisciplinary research biases.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10407,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9819,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Citation Impact**\n\nI am now focusing on two key falsification scenarios involving citations and funding. I'll test whether maximally distant domain-bridging proposals actually show higher funding success and faster citation uptake, going against conventional notions. Also, I'll audit citation data to see if a surge in paper volume indeed decreases the Gini coefficient and accelerates the integration of peripheral lemmas, contradicting current theories.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10682,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 10094,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Falsification Strategies**\n\nI'm now focusing on refining falsification strategies by shifting from theoretical impossibility to practical applicability. I'm aiming for sample-based estimators that accurately predict total support size within 10% across uniform and truncated tails. Crucially, I'm reframing falsification around the empirical applicability of assumptions to real candidate generators, checking this against real fuzzer runs. The goal is to show the failure of smoothed Good–Toulmin or the accuracy of GPD/Chao1.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 10929,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 10341,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-carbon","responseId": "2gMDa-blDJbmwbUPm4_kqQ0"}

